# key: (title, hook, big idea, keys [[label, math]], method steps, traps, explain-it-another-way)
L = {}
L['matter'] = ('Classifying matter and changes',
 'A golf ball is a mixture of materials (a rubber core, a plastic cover), but the water in your bottle is a pure compound. Chemists sort everything the same way: pure, or mixed?',
 'Matter is either a pure substance (an element or a compound, with a fixed composition) or a mixture (homogeneous or heterogeneous). Physical changes alter form; chemical changes make new substances.',
 [['Element', r'\text{one kind of atom: Cu, O₂, He}'], ['Compound', r'\text{elements bonded in a fixed ratio: H₂O, NaCl}'], ['Homogeneous', r'\text{uniform throughout: salt water, air, brass}'], ['Heterogeneous', r'\text{visibly uneven: granite, soup, trail mix}']],
 ['Ask: is it one substance, or more than one?', 'One substance: one kind of atom means an element; a fixed formula means a compound.', 'A mixture: the same throughout is homogeneous; visibly different parts is heterogeneous.', 'For a change, ask whether a new substance forms. Gas, a color change, light, heat, a new smell or a new solid mean chemical.'],
 ['Salt water looks pure, but it is a homogeneous mixture: boil off the water and the salt is left behind.', 'Melting, boiling and dissolving are physical changes; the substance is still the same.', 'Properties you can see without changing the substance (density, boiling point, color) are physical. Flammability and reactivity are chemical.'],
 'Think of your golf bag. Each club is its own pure thing, like an element or compound: a 7-iron is always a 7-iron. The bag full of clubs, balls and tees is a mixture: you can take it apart by hand, and every bag is packed differently. Cleaning your clubs is a physical change; rust on an old wedge is chemical, because iron oxide is a new substance.')
L['sigfig'] = ('Significant figures and scientific notation',
 'A rangefinder that reads 152.4 yards promises more precision than one that reads 150. Significant figures are how a number tells you how precisely it was measured.',
 'Significant figures are the digits you actually measured, plus one estimated digit. A calculated answer can\'t be more precise than its least precise measurement.',
 [['Always count', r'\text{nonzero digits, and zeros between them (1007 has 4)}'], ['Never count', r'\text{leading zeros (0.0045 has 2)}'], ['Trailing zeros', r'\text{count only with a decimal point: 25.00 has 4, 2500 has 2}'], ['× and ÷', r'\text{round to the fewest significant figures}'], ['+ and −', r'\text{round to the fewest decimal places}']],
 ['To count, start at the first nonzero digit and count to the end. Drop trailing zeros only when there is no decimal point.', 'Multiplying or dividing: keep the fewest significant figures of the measurements.', 'Adding or subtracting: keep the fewest decimal places.', 'Scientific notation: put one nonzero digit before the decimal point, then × 10 to the number of places you moved it. Moving left gives a positive exponent.'],
 ['Leading zeros (0.00450) only hold the decimal point; they never count.', 'Don\'t use the decimal-place rule for multiplying, or the significant-figure rule for adding.', 'Round once, at the end. Rounding every step makes the answer drift.'],
 'Picture yardages. A caddie who says "about 150" gives two significant figures; "152.4 yards" gives four. If you add a 152.4-yard carry to "roughly 20" yards of roll, the total is only as precise as the rough guess: the sloppiest number limits the answer. Leading zeros, as in 0.004, just say "zero ones, zero tenths". They hold the decimal point in place.')
L['convert'] = ('Metric conversions and dimensional analysis',
 'Courses list holes in yards, but launch monitors often report meters. Converting units is just multiplying by a fraction that equals 1.',
 'Multiply by conversion factors written so the unit you don\'t want cancels. Each factor equals one, so the amount stays the same; only the units change.',
 [['Prefixes', r'\text{k}=10^{3},\;\text{c}=10^{-2},\;\text{m}=10^{-3},\;\text{µ}=10^{-6},\;\text{n}=10^{-9}'], ['A factor', r'\f{1000\text{ m}}{1\text{ km}}=1'], ['Squared, cubed', r'1\text{ m}^2=10^4\text{ cm}^2,\;1\text{ mL}=1\text{ cm}^3']],
 ['Write the starting amount with its unit.', 'Multiply by a factor with the unwanted unit on the bottom and the new unit on top.', 'Chain more factors if needed, until only the unit you want is left.', 'Multiply the tops, divide by the bottoms. Exact definitions (1 km = 1000 m) don\'t limit significant figures.'],
 ['Going to a smaller unit makes the number bigger: 2.5 km is 2500 m.', 'For cm² or m³, square or cube the conversion factor too.', 'If the units don\'t cancel, flip the factor.'],
 'Think of it as exchanging currency. You hand over yards and get meters at a fixed rate (1 yd = 0.9144 m). Write the rate as a fraction with yards on the bottom so they cancel out. If you need several exchanges (days to hours to minutes), keep trading until you hold the unit you want.')
L['density'] = ('Density and percent error',
 'A golf ball and a ping-pong ball are about the same size, but the golf ball is far heavier. That is density: how much mass is packed into each bit of volume.',
 'Density = mass ÷ volume. It doesn\'t depend on the size of the sample, so it can identify a substance. Percent error measures how far a measurement is from the accepted value.',
 [['Density', r'D=\f{m}{V}'], ['Displacement', r'V=V_{\text{final}}-V_{\text{initial}}'], ['Percent error', r'\f{|\text{measured}-\text{accepted}|}{\text{accepted}}\times100']],
 ['Write D = m/V and fill in what you know.', 'Rearrange for the unknown: m = D × V, or V = m ÷ D.', 'For an odd-shaped solid, find its volume by water displacement: final reading minus starting reading.', 'Percent error: subtract, drop the sign, divide by the accepted value, multiply by 100.'],
 ['Divide mass by volume, not volume by mass.', 'With displacement, the object\'s volume is the RISE in water level, not the final reading.', 'Percent error divides by the accepted value, not the measured one.'],
 'Imagine two range buckets, both full. One holds golf balls, the other ping-pong balls. Same volume, very different mass, so the golf-ball bucket is denser. Density doesn\'t care about the size of the bucket: half a bucket of golf balls is exactly as dense as a full one.')
L['models'] = ('Atomic models and subatomic particles',
 'Golf club design improved one test at a time, and so did our model of the atom: from solid balls to a nucleus wrapped in electron clouds.',
 'Each experiment changed the model: Dalton\'s solid atoms, Thomson\'s electrons, Rutherford\'s dense positive nucleus, Bohr\'s energy levels, and today\'s quantum orbitals. Protons and neutrons sit in the nucleus; electrons are outside it.',
 [['Proton', r'+1,\;1\text{ amu, in the nucleus}'], ['Neutron', r'0,\;1\text{ amu, in the nucleus}'], ['Electron', r'-1,\;\approx0\text{ amu, outside the nucleus}']],
 ['Dalton: atoms are tiny and indivisible, and each element has its own kind.', 'Thomson (cathode rays): atoms contain negative electrons, the plum-pudding model.', 'Rutherford (gold foil): most alpha particles went straight through, but a few bounced back. So the atom is mostly empty space with a tiny, dense, positive nucleus.', 'Bohr: electrons in fixed energy levels. The quantum model: electrons in orbitals, regions of probability.'],
 ['Protons, not neutrons or electrons, decide which element it is.', 'Nearly all the mass is in the nucleus; electrons take up space, not mass.', 'The gold-foil experiment revealed the nucleus. Energy levels came later, from Bohr.'],
 'Picture a golf course from a blimp. Almost everything is open fairway and rough, but the clubhouse in the middle holds nearly all the mass. That is Rutherford\'s atom: a tiny, heavy nucleus with electrons spread far around it. Most alpha particles sailed through the empty space like shots over open fairway; only the rare one hit the clubhouse and bounced back.')
L['isotope'] = ('Isotopes, ions and counting particles',
 'Two golf balls can look identical but have different cores. Isotopes are atoms of the same element with different numbers of neutrons.',
 'Atomic number (Z) = protons. Mass number (A) = protons + neutrons. Charge = protons − electrons.',
 [['Protons', r'Z'], ['Neutrons', r'A-Z'], ['Electrons', r'Z-\text{charge}'], ['Notation', r'\ce{^{56}_{26}Fe^3+}']],
 ['Protons = the atomic number: the bottom number, or from the periodic table.', 'Neutrons = mass number − atomic number.', 'Electrons = protons for a neutral atom. For an ion, subtract the charge: a 2+ ion has 2 fewer electrons, a 3− ion has 3 more.', 'Isotopes have the same protons but different neutrons, like carbon-12 and carbon-14.'],
 ['The mass number counts protons AND neutrons: subtract the atomic number to get neutrons.', 'Positive ions have LOST electrons; negative ions have GAINED them.', 'Change protons and you change the element; change electrons and you make an ion; change neutrons and you make an isotope.'],
 'Think of a team. The protons are the team name: change them and it is a different team (element). Neutrons are bench players: add one or two and it is the same team, just heavier (an isotope). Electrons are fans in the stands: gain or lose a few and the team picks up a charge (an ion), but it is still the same team.')
L['avgmass'] = ('Average atomic mass',
 'Your average score isn\'t the average of your best and worst rounds; it depends on how often you shoot each one. Average atomic mass works the same way.',
 'Average atomic mass is a weighted average of the isotopes\' masses, weighted by how common each isotope is (its percent abundance).',
 [['Weighted average', r'\text{sum of (mass × fraction)}'], ['Fraction', r'75.78\%\to0.7578'], ['Reverse problem', r'm_1x+m_2(1-x)=\text{average}']],
 ['Change each percent abundance to a decimal.', 'Multiply each isotope\'s mass by its decimal abundance.', 'Add the products. The answer lands closest to the most common isotope.', 'To find an abundance, let x be one fraction and 1 − x the other, then solve.'],
 ['A plain average of the masses ignores how common each isotope is.', 'Multiply by decimals (0.7578), not percents (75.78).', 'Use the exact isotope masses given, not the rounded mass numbers.'],
 'Say you shoot 72 most weekends (75% of rounds) and 80 on bad days (25%). Your average isn\'t 76; it is 0.75 × 72 + 0.25 × 80 = 74, pulled toward the score you shoot most. Chlorine is the same: mostly chlorine-35 with some chlorine-37, so its average, 35.45, sits close to 35.')
L['light'] = ('Light and energy',
 'On a launch monitor, more club speed means more energy into the ball. For light, higher frequency means more energy in each photon.',
 'Light travels at c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m/s. Wavelength and frequency are inversely related (c = λν), and a photon\'s energy rises with frequency (E = hν).',
 [['Waves', r'c=\lambda\nu'], ['Energy', r'E=h\nu=\f{hc}{\lambda}'], ['Constants', r'c=3.00\times10^{8}\text{ m/s},\;h=6.626\times10^{-34}\text{ J·s}'], ['Spectrum', r'\text{radio → gamma: energy up, wavelength down}']],
 ['Convert wavelength to meters (1 nm = 10⁻⁹ m).', 'Frequency: ν = c ÷ λ. Wavelength: λ = c ÷ ν.', 'Energy: E = hν, or E = hc ÷ λ in one step.', 'Comparing light: shorter wavelength means higher frequency and more energy (violet over red, X-rays over radio).'],
 ['Convert nanometers to meters before using c = λν.', 'Wavelength and frequency are inversely related: double one and the other halves.', 'E = hν multiplies; don\'t divide by h.'],
 'Picture waves on a pond. If the crests are close together (short wavelength), many pass you each second (high frequency), and each one hits harder (more energy). Long, lazy radio waves are gentle; short gamma waves pack a punch. When an electron drops a long way between energy levels, it releases a high-energy photon, like a long downhill putt picking up speed.')
L['config'] = ('Electron configurations',
 'Players tee off in a set order, and electrons fill sublevels in a set order too: lowest energy first.',
 'Electrons fill sublevels in order of energy: 1s 2s 2p 3s 3p 4s 3d 4p 5s 4d 5p. An s sublevel holds 2, p holds 6, d holds 10. Valence electrons are the outermost s and p electrons.',
 [['Filling order', r'\text{1s 2s 2p 3s 3p 4s 3d 4p 5s 4d 5p}'], ['Capacity', r's=2,\;p=6,\;d=10,\;f=14'], ['Shorthand', r'\cfg{[Ne] 3s2}\text{ is magnesium}'], ['Valence', r'\text{groups 1–2: 1–2; groups 13–18: 3–8}']],
 ['Count the electrons: the atomic number, adjusted for any ion charge.', 'Fill sublevels in order, writing the number of electrons as an exponent.', 'For shorthand, start with the previous noble gas in brackets.', 'Check that the exponents add up to the electron count.'],
 ['4s fills before 3d, and after 4s comes 3d, not 4d.', 'For ions, remove electrons for a positive charge and add them for a negative one.', 'A p sublevel holds 6 electrons, not 8.'],
 'Think of a parking garage. The ground level (1s) fills first with 2 cars, then the next levels in order. One upper level (4s) is easier to reach than a lower wing (3d), so it fills first: that is why 4s comes before 3d. Valence electrons are the cars on the top level, the ones that leave or arrive when atoms react.')
L['table'] = ('The periodic table',
 'Golf sorts clubs into woods, irons and wedges because each family behaves alike. The periodic table sorts elements into families the same way.',
 'Rows are periods (energy levels); columns are groups, families with similar properties. Metals are on the left, nonmetals on the right, and metalloids along the staircase line.',
 [['Group 1', r'\text{alkali metals: very reactive}'], ['Group 2', r'\text{alkaline earth metals}'], ['Groups 3–12', r'\text{transition metals}'], ['Groups 17, 18', r'\text{halogens; noble gases}']],
 ['Find the period (row): it matches the outermost energy level.', 'Find the group (column 1–18): elements in a group share valence electrons and behave alike.', 'Left of the staircase: metals. Right of it: nonmetals. Along it: metalloids (B, Si, Ge, As, Sb, Te).', 'The table is ordered by atomic number (Moseley), not by atomic mass.'],
 ['Periods are rows; groups are columns.', 'Hydrogen sits in group 1 but is a nonmetal.', 'Noble gases are unreactive because their outer energy level is full.'],
 'Think of a golf bag sorted by club family. Every wedge has a short shaft and a lofted face; every alkali metal has one valence electron and reacts with water. Move down a column and you get bigger versions of the same family; move across a row and you change families.')
L['trends'] = ('Periodic trends',
 'A firmer grip pulls the club in tighter. A nucleus with more protons pulls its electrons in tighter, and that explains every periodic trend.',
 'Across a period, more protons pull the same energy level inward: atoms shrink, and ionization energy and electronegativity rise. Down a group, extra energy levels make atoms bigger and their outer electrons easier to remove.',
 [['Atomic radius', r'\text{bigger down a group and to the left}'], ['Ionization energy', r'\text{higher up a group and to the right}'], ['Electronegativity', r'\text{higher up and to the right; F is highest}'], ['Ions', r'\text{cations are smaller, anions larger, than their atoms}']],
 ['Locate both elements: same period, or same group?', 'Same period: the one further right has more protons pulling inward, so it is smaller with higher ionization energy and electronegativity.', 'Same group: the one further down has more energy levels, so it is bigger with lower ionization energy and electronegativity.', 'For ions with the same number of electrons, more protons means a smaller ion.'],
 ['Across a period atoms get SMALLER, even though they gain electrons.', 'Metallic character runs opposite to ionization energy: it grows down and to the left.', 'A positive ion is smaller than its atom; a negative ion is larger.'],
 'Imagine the nucleus as a player holding a rope with electrons on the end. Across a period the player gets stronger (more protons) and reels the electrons in: a smaller atom that is harder to steal from. Down a group, each new energy level is a longer rope: the electrons sit farther out and slip away more easily.')
L['ionic'] = ('Ions and ionic formulas',
 'In a team match, the sides have to balance. In an ionic compound, the positive and negative charges have to balance to zero.',
 'Metals lose electrons to form positive ions; nonmetals gain electrons to form negative ions. An ionic formula combines ions so the total charge is zero, in the lowest whole-number ratio.',
 [['Main-group charges', r'\text{groups 1, 2, 13: +1, +2, +3; groups 15, 16, 17: −3, −2, −1}'], ['Criss-cross', r'\ce{Al^3+}\text{ and }\ce{O^2-}\to\ce{Al2O3}'], ['Polyatomic ions', r'\text{keep them whole; parentheses for more than one}']],
 ['Write each ion with its charge.', 'Criss-cross the charge numbers into subscripts.', 'Reduce to the lowest whole-number ratio.', 'Put parentheses around a polyatomic ion when there is more than one of it, as in Ca₃(PO₄)₂.'],
 ['The charges must cancel: Mg²⁺ and Cl⁻ make MgCl₂, not MgCl.', 'Never change the subscripts inside a polyatomic ion.', 'Cl₂ is diatomic only as an element. In NaCl, each Cl⁻ counts once.'],
 'Think of a team match where both sides must end level. Aluminum brings +3 per atom and oxygen brings −2. Two aluminums make +6 and three oxygens make −6: level, so Al₂O₃. The criss-cross trick just finds the smallest numbers that make the totals match.')
L['lewis'] = ('Lewis structures',
 'Before a shot you check your yardage. Before drawing a molecule, you count its valence electrons.',
 'A Lewis structure shows valence electrons as bonds (shared pairs) and lone pairs, so each atom except hydrogen ends up with eight.',
 [['Valence electrons', r'\text{H 1, C 4, N 5, O 6, halogens 7}'], ['Ions', r'\text{add one per negative charge; remove one per positive}'], ['Octet', r'\text{8 around each atom (H: 2)}']],
 ['Count the valence electrons of every atom, and adjust for an ion\'s charge.', 'Put the least electronegative atom in the center (never H) and connect the others with single bonds.', 'Fill the outer atoms\' octets with lone pairs.', 'Put any leftover electrons on the central atom. If it still has fewer than eight, turn lone pairs into double or triple bonds.'],
 ['Count every atom: CO₂ has 4 + 6 + 6 = 16 valence electrons, not 10.', 'Add electrons for a negative ion; subtract them for a positive one.', 'Count lone PAIRS, not single electrons.'],
 'Think of the valence electrons as a budget of golf balls: you must use exactly that many. Each bond spends two balls shared by two players; each lone pair is two balls one player keeps. Everyone except hydrogen wants eight in their bag, so you trade lone pairs for double bonds until the budget balances.')
L['shape'] = ('Shapes, bond type and polarity',
 'Players on a crowded tee box spread out to stay out of each other\'s way. Electron groups around an atom spread out the same way.',
 'Electron groups (bonds and lone pairs) repel and spread as far apart as possible (VSEPR). The electronegativity difference sets the bond type, and the shape decides whether polar bonds cancel.',
 [['2, 3, 4 groups', r'\text{linear, trigonal planar, tetrahedral}'], ['With lone pairs', r'\text{trigonal pyramidal (3 + 1), bent (2 + 1 or 2 + 2)}'], ['Electronegativity gap', r'<0.4\text{ nonpolar},\;0.4\text{–}1.7\text{ polar},\;>1.7\text{ ionic}'], ['Polar molecule', r'\text{polar bonds and an unsymmetric shape}']],
 ['Draw the Lewis structure and count electron groups on the central atom.', 'Name the shape from the bonded atoms only; lone pairs bend it.', 'For bond type, subtract the electronegativities.', 'For polarity: symmetric shapes with identical outer atoms cancel (nonpolar). Lone pairs or different outer atoms make it polar.'],
 ['NH₃ has four electron groups arranged tetrahedrally, but its shape is trigonal pyramidal.', 'A molecule can have polar bonds and still be nonpolar (CO₂, CCl₄).', 'Lone pairs squeeze bond angles: 109.5°, then 107°, then 104.5°.'],
 'Picture balloons tied together at the knots. Two point opposite ways (linear), three make a flat triangle, four make a tetrahedron. Now make one balloon invisible: that is a lone pair. It still takes up space, but you only see the other three, so the shape looks like a pyramid. Polarity is a tug-of-war: if equal pullers pull in symmetric directions, the rope doesn\'t move.')
L['imf'] = ('Intermolecular forces',
 'Teammates can be strong on their own, but how well they stick together decides a scramble. Molecules feel forces between each other, not just the bonds inside them.',
 'Intermolecular forces hold molecules together: London dispersion forces (in every molecule, stronger for bigger ones), dipole–dipole forces (polar molecules) and hydrogen bonding (H on N, O or F, the strongest). Stronger forces mean higher boiling points.',
 [['London dispersion', r'\text{every molecule; stronger for bigger ones}'], ['Dipole–dipole', r'\text{polar molecules}'], ['Hydrogen bonding', r'\text{H bonded to N, O or F}'], ['Boiling point', r'\text{stronger forces → higher boiling point}']],
 ['Decide whether the molecule is polar, from its shape.', 'Look for H bonded directly to N, O or F: that means hydrogen bonding.', 'Polar without that: dipole–dipole. Nonpolar: London dispersion only.', 'To compare boiling points: the stronger strongest force wins. If both have the same kind, the bigger molecule wins.'],
 ['Hydrogen bonding needs H on N, O or F. HCl doesn\'t have it.', 'Ionic and covalent bonds are inside compounds, not between molecules.', 'Bigger nonpolar molecules (I₂ versus F₂) have stronger London forces.'],
 'Think of molecules as golfers leaving the 18th green. Nonpolar ones barely notice each other (weak London forces) and scatter fast: a low boiling point. Polar ones chat in pairs (dipole–dipole). Hydrogen-bonded ones, like water, link arms and don\'t want to leave, so it takes a lot of energy, 100 °C, to pull them apart into a gas.')
L['nameion'] = ('Naming ionic compounds',
 'A scorecard lists every player by full name, not a nickname. Ionic compounds have official names too: cation first, then anion.',
 'Name the cation, then the anion. Single-atom anions end in -ide; polyatomic ions keep their own names; metals that can have more than one charge get a Roman numeral.',
 [['Two elements', r'\text{NaCl: sodium chloride}'], ['Roman numeral', r'\text{Fe₂O₃: iron(III) oxide}'], ['Polyatomic', r'\text{CaCO₃: calcium carbonate}']],
 ['Name the cation: the metal\'s name (with a Roman numeral for Fe, Cu, Pb, Sn, Co), or ammonium.', 'Name the anion: the element\'s root + -ide, or the polyatomic ion\'s name.', 'For the Roman numeral, use the anions\' total charge to work out the metal\'s charge.', 'From a name to a formula: write both ions, balance the charges, and add parentheses for more than one polyatomic ion.'],
 ['The Roman numeral is the metal\'s charge, not its subscript: Fe₂O₃ is iron(III) oxide.', 'No prefixes (di-, tri-) in ionic names.', '-ate and -ite ions differ by one oxygen: nitrate is NO₃⁻, nitrite is NO₂⁻.'],
 'Treat the name like introducing a pairing: the positive player first, then the negative one. If the first player could carry more than one handicap (iron can be 2+ or 3+), you announce which in Roman numerals. A polyatomic ion is a team with a fixed name: sulfate is always SO₄²⁻, so you never rename it.')
L['namecov'] = ('Naming molecular compounds and acids',
 'Golf counts everything: a three-putt, a double bogey. Molecular names use counting prefixes too.',
 'Two nonmetals: use prefixes (mono, di, tri, tetra, penta, hexa…) for how many of each atom, and drop "mono" on the first element. Acids: binary acids are hydro-…-ic acid; -ate ions make -ic acids; -ite ions make -ous acids.',
 [['Prefixes', r'\text{mono 1, di 2, tri 3, tetra 4, penta 5, hexa 6, hepta 7, deca 10}'], ['Example', r'\text{N₂O₄: dinitrogen tetroxide}'], ['Acids', r'\text{HCl hydrochloric; HNO₃ nitric; HNO₂ nitrous}']],
 ['Check: are both elements nonmetals? Then use prefixes.', 'First element: its name, with a prefix only if there is more than one atom.', 'Second element: prefix + root + -ide (monoxide, not monooxide).', 'Acids: no oxygen means hydro + root + -ic acid. An -ate ion gives an -ic acid; an -ite ion gives an -ous acid.'],
 ['Never reduce a molecular formula: N₂O₄ is not NO₂.', 'Hydro- is only for binary acids like HCl. H₂SO₄ is sulfuric acid, not hydrosulfuric.', 'Roman numerals are for ionic compounds, never molecular ones.'],
 'Read the name like a scorecard: "dinitrogen tetroxide" says two nitrogens, four oxygens, N₂O₄. The prefixes are the stroke counts, so you never simplify them. For acids: "-ate, I ate something icky" (nitrate becomes nitric acid); "-ite is delight-ous" (nitrite becomes nitrous acid).')
L['balance'] = ('Balancing equations',
 'A scorecard has to add up: the front nine plus the back nine equals the total. A chemical equation has to add up too: atoms in equal atoms out.',
 'Atoms are conserved, so every element must have the same count on both sides. Change only coefficients, never subscripts, and use the lowest whole numbers.',
 [['Conservation', r'\text{atoms in = atoms out}'], ['Example', r'\ce{2H2 + O2 -> 2H2O}'], ['Order', r'\text{complicated formulas first; lone elements and O last}']],
 ['Count the atoms of each element on both sides.', 'Balance the element in the most complicated formula first. Leave lone elements (O₂, H₂, Fe) for last.', 'A polyatomic ion that appears unchanged on both sides can be balanced as one unit.', 'Recount everything, and divide by a common factor if every coefficient has one.'],
 ['Never change a subscript: that changes the substance.', 'Balanced but not in lowest terms (4, 2, 4) still needs dividing.', 'Combustion: balance C, then H, then O.'],
 'Balancing is like settling up after a round: the money everyone had at the start must equal what they have at the end. Coefficients are how many of each bet you place. You can place more bets, but you can\'t change what one bet is worth (the subscripts).')
L['rxtype'] = ('Reaction types and predicting products',
 'Golf has match play, stroke play and scrambles, each with its own pattern. Reactions come in a few patterns too.',
 'Synthesis (A + B → AB), decomposition (AB → A + B), single replacement (A + BC → AC + B), double replacement (AB + CD → AD + CB) and combustion (fuel + O₂ → CO₂ + H₂O). Spot the pattern and you can predict the products.',
 [['Synthesis', r'A+B\to AB'], ['Decomposition', r'AB\to A+B'], ['Single replacement', r'A+BC\to AC+B'], ['Double replacement', r'AB+CD\to AD+CB'], ['Combustion', r'\text{fuel}+\ce{O2}\to\ce{CO2}+\ce{H2O}']],
 ['Count the reactants and products, and notice which are elements and which are compounds.', 'Single replacement happens only if the free element is more active (activity series).', 'Double replacement: swap the partners, then fix each new formula\'s charges.', 'Combustion of a hydrocarbon always makes CO₂ and H₂O.'],
 ['In double replacement, cations pair with anions, never cation with cation.', 'A less active metal can\'t replace a more active one: no reaction.', 'Rebalance the charges in new compounds: Pb²⁺ with I⁻ makes PbI₂.'],
 'Think of changing playing partners. Single replacement is one player stepping in to take another\'s place, and only a stronger player can. Double replacement is two pairs swapping partners. Synthesis is players forming a team; decomposition is the team splitting up. Combustion is the clubhouse fire pit: fuel plus oxygen gives CO₂ and water.')
L['molar'] = ('Molar mass and mole conversions',
 'A dozen balls is always 12 balls, whatever the brand. A mole is always 6.02 × 10²³ particles, whatever the substance.',
 'A mole is 6.02 × 10²³ particles. Molar mass (g/mol) is the mass of one mole: add up the atomic masses in the formula. It converts between grams and moles.',
 [['Avogadro\'s number', r'1\text{ mol}=6.02\times10^{23}\text{ particles}'], ['Grams to moles', r'n=\f{\text{mass}}{\text{molar mass}}'], ['Molar mass', r'\ce{H2O}:\;2(1.01)+16.00=18.02\text{ g/mol}']],
 ['Molar mass: count each atom (a subscript after parentheses multiplies everything inside) and add the atomic masses.', 'Grams to moles: divide by the molar mass.', 'Moles to grams: multiply by the molar mass.', 'Moles to particles: multiply by 6.02 × 10²³. For atoms of one element, also multiply by its subscript.'],
 ['A subscript after parentheses multiplies everything inside: Ca(NO₃)₂ has 2 N and 6 O.', 'Use atomic masses, not atomic numbers.', 'Moles to particles multiplies by Avogadro\'s number; particles to moles divides.'],
 'A mole is a counting word, like a dozen. A dozen golf balls and a dozen bowling balls are the same count but very different weights. One mole of carbon weighs 12.01 g; one mole of gold weighs 196.97 g. Molar mass just answers: how much does a mole-sized bag of this stuff weigh?')
L['pcomp'] = ('Percent composition and hydrates',
 'Your stats might say 40% of your strokes are putts. Percent composition says what share of a compound\'s mass comes from each element.',
 'Percent by mass = (mass of the element in one mole ÷ molar mass) × 100. Hydrates hold water in a fixed ratio; heating drives it off.',
 [['Percent by mass', r'\f{(\text{subscript})(\text{atomic mass})}{\text{molar mass}}\times100'], ['Hydrate', r'\text{CuSO₄·5H₂O: 5 water per formula unit}'], ['Finding x', r'x=\f{\text{mol water}}{\text{mol salt}}']],
 ['Find the molar mass of the compound.', 'Multiply the element\'s atomic mass by its subscript.', 'Divide by the molar mass and multiply by 100.', 'Hydrate formula: water lost and salt left, each converted to moles; x is the ratio.'],
 ['Percent composition is by MASS, not by number of atoms.', 'Include the subscript: 2 × 1.01 for H₂.', 'To find x in a hydrate, compare moles, not grams.'],
 'If a round of 80 included 32 putts, putts are 32 ÷ 80 = 40% of your strokes. Percent composition splits mass the same way: in water, oxygen supplies 16.00 of every 18.02 g, so water is 88.8% oxygen by mass even though only one of its three atoms is oxygen.')
L['empir'] = ('Empirical and molecular formulas',
 'A practice plan of 30 putts, 15 chips and 45 full swings has a simplest ratio of 2 : 1 : 3. An empirical formula is a compound\'s simplest atom ratio.',
 'The empirical formula is the simplest whole-number ratio of atoms. The molecular formula is a whole-number multiple of it: C₆H₁₂O₆ is 6 × CH₂O.',
 [['Percent to grams', r'\text{assume a 100 g sample}'], ['Grams to ratio', r'\text{÷ atomic mass, then ÷ the smallest}'], ['Molecular formula', r'n=\f{\text{molar mass}}{\text{empirical formula mass}}']],
 ['Assume 100 g, so each percent becomes grams.', 'Divide each by its atomic mass to get moles.', 'Divide every mole value by the smallest one.', 'If you get x.5 or x.33, multiply everything by 2 or 3. For the molecular formula, multiply the subscripts by molar mass ÷ empirical mass.'],
 ['Mass percents aren\'t mole ratios: divide by atomic masses first.', 'Don\'t round 1.5 to 2; multiply every ratio by 2 instead.', 'The empirical formula is the molecular formula only when the multiplier is 1.'],
 'Think of the practice plan again: 30 putts, 15 chips and 45 full swings reduce to 2 : 1 : 3. That is the empirical formula. The actual session, the molecular formula, is that ratio times 15. Finding the multiplier is just "total ÷ one set".')
L['stoich'] = ('Stoichiometry',
 'A lesson plan might say "two buckets of balls for every lesson". A balanced equation says how many moles of each substance go together.',
 'The coefficients of a balanced equation are mole ratios. Grams can\'t go straight through an equation: convert to moles, use the ratio, then convert back.',
 [['Mole ratio', r'\f{\text{mol wanted}}{\text{mol given}}'], ['The path', r'\text{g A → mol A → mol B → g B}']],
 ['Make sure the equation is balanced.', 'Convert the given grams to moles (÷ molar mass).', 'Multiply by the mole ratio: the wanted coefficient over the given coefficient.', 'Convert moles to grams of the wanted substance (× its molar mass).'],
 ['Coefficients are MOLE ratios, not gram ratios.', 'Put what you want on top of the mole ratio.', 'Don\'t stop at moles when the question asks for grams.'],
 'Think of a recipe: 2 eggs for every 1 cup of flour. With 3 cups of flour you need 6 eggs; that is the mole ratio. But here the store sells everything by weight, so first convert grams to "how many" (moles), use the recipe ratio, then convert back to grams.')
L['limit'] = ('Limiting reactant and percent yield',
 'You can\'t play more holes than you have balls for. The reactant that runs out first limits how much product you can make.',
 'The limiting reactant is used up first and sets the theoretical yield. Percent yield compares what you actually collected with that maximum.',
 [['Limiting reactant', r'\text{the one that makes the least product}'], ['Percent yield', r'\f{\text{actual}}{\text{theoretical}}\times100'], ['Excess left', r'\text{starting amount} - \text{amount used}']],
 ['Convert each reactant to moles.', 'Work out how much product each one could make (or divide each one\'s moles by its coefficient).', 'The smaller amount is the theoretical yield, and that reactant is limiting.', 'Percent yield = actual ÷ theoretical × 100.'],
 ['Don\'t compare grams of reactants; compare moles adjusted by the coefficients.', 'The theoretical yield comes from the limiting reactant, not the one in excess.', 'Percent yield is actual ÷ theoretical, not the other way round.'],
 'You head out with 14 clubs and 3 balls. The clubs aren\'t the problem: you will run out of balls first, so the balls are the limiting reactant. However the round goes, the number of balls decides how much golf you play; the extra clubs are in excess.')
L['pressure'] = ('Pressure, temperature and kinetic theory',
 'A golf ball flies farther on a hot day partly because warm air is thinner: its molecules move faster and spread out. Gases are all about particles in motion.',
 'Gas particles move fast and randomly. Pressure comes from their collisions with the walls, and temperature (in kelvin) measures their average kinetic energy.',
 [['Pressure units', r'1\text{ atm}=101.3\text{ kPa}=760\text{ mmHg}=14.7\text{ psi}'], ['Kelvin', r'K=°C+273'], ['STP', r'0\text{ °C (273 K) and 1 atm}']],
 ['Convert pressures with 1 atm = 101.3 kPa = 760 mmHg = 14.7 psi.', 'Convert temperatures to kelvin by adding 273.', 'Kinetic-molecular theory: particles are tiny, far apart and always moving, with elastic collisions and no attractions.', 'Real gases behave most like ideal gases at high temperature and low pressure.'],
 ['Gas-law temperatures must be in kelvin.', 'Temperature measures the AVERAGE kinetic energy of the particles.', 'At the same temperature, lighter gases move faster.'],
 'Picture a driving range inside a closed dome. The balls (gas particles) fly everywhere and bang into the walls: that is pressure. Turn up the heat and they fly faster, hitting the walls harder and more often. Absolute zero, 0 K, would be every ball lying still.')
L['gaslaw'] = ('Boyle, Charles and the combined gas law',
 'A golf ball squashes at impact and springs back. A gas\'s volume, pressure and temperature trade off in predictable ways.',
 'With the amount of gas fixed: pressure and volume are inversely related (Boyle), volume and kelvin temperature are directly related (Charles), and pressure and temperature are directly related (Gay-Lussac). The combined gas law handles all three.',
 [["Boyle's law", r'P_1V_1=P_2V_2'], ["Charles's law", r'\f{V_1}{T_1}=\f{V_2}{T_2}'], ["Gay-Lussac's law", r'\f{P_1}{T_1}=\f{P_2}{T_2}'], ['Combined', r'\f{P_1V_1}{T_1}=\f{P_2V_2}{T_2}']],
 ['List what is given and what stays constant.', 'Convert every temperature to kelvin.', 'Choose the law, or use the combined law and cross out what is constant.', 'Solve, then check the direction makes sense (hotter gas at the same pressure should get bigger).'],
 ['Using °C instead of K gives wrong, even negative, answers.', 'Boyle\'s law is inverse: double the pressure and the volume halves.', 'Charles\'s and Gay-Lussac\'s laws are direct: hotter means bigger or higher pressure.'],
 'Squeeze a balloon (raise the pressure) and it shrinks: Boyle. Leave a balloon in a hot car and it swells: Charles. Leave a sealed can of tennis balls in the sun and the pressure inside climbs: Gay-Lussac. The combined law is all three at once. Just use kelvin, because 0 °C isn\'t zero energy.')
L['ideal'] = ('The ideal gas law',
 'One formula, four dials. PV = nRT connects pressure, volume, moles and temperature for any gas.',
 'PV = nRT. With P in atm, V in liters, n in moles and T in kelvin, R = 0.0821 L·atm/(mol·K).',
 [['Ideal gas law', r'PV=nRT'], ['R', r'0.0821\text{ L·atm/(mol·K)}'], ['Molar mass', r'M=\f{\text{grams}}{n}']],
 ['Convert to atm, liters, moles and kelvin.', 'Rearrange PV = nRT for the unknown.', 'Substitute and solve.', 'With grams, convert to moles first (n = grams ÷ molar mass), or find a molar mass from M = grams ÷ n.'],
 ['Use kelvin, never °C.', 'Match R to the units: 0.0821 goes with atm and liters.', 'n is moles, not grams.'],
 'Think of PV = nRT as a balanced scorecard: pressure × volume on one side must equal moles × R × temperature on the other. Change any one number and something else has to give. R is just the conversion number that makes the units line up.')
L['dalton'] = ('Partial pressures and gas stoichiometry',
 'A foursome\'s team total is just the four scores added up. Gas mixtures work the same way: the partial pressures add up to the total.',
 'Each gas in a mixture contributes its own partial pressure, and they add up to the total (Dalton\'s law). At STP, one mole of any gas takes up 22.4 L.',
 [["Dalton's law", r'P_{\text{total}}=P_1+P_2+P_3'], ['Mole fraction', r'P_A=X_A\,P_{\text{total}}'], ['Over water', r'P_{\text{gas}}=P_{\text{total}}-P_{\text{water}}'], ['At STP', r'1\text{ mol}=22.4\text{ L}']],
 ['Add the partial pressures for the total, or subtract to find a missing one.', 'For gas collected over water, subtract the water\'s vapor pressure.', 'Partial pressure = mole fraction × total pressure.', 'Gas stoichiometry at STP: grams → moles → mole ratio → moles × 22.4 L.'],
 ['Over water, SUBTRACT the vapor pressure; don\'t add it.', 'The mole fraction is moles of A ÷ TOTAL moles.', '22.4 L per mole only holds at STP.'],
 'Picture each gas as a golfer adding strokes to the team total. Nitrogen supplies most of air\'s pressure because most of the "players" are nitrogen. If water vapor sneaks into your collection tube, it adds strokes you didn\'t make: subtract it to get the dry gas\'s score.')
L['solub'] = ('Solutions and solubility',
 'Sports-drink powder disappears into water; sunscreen doesn\'t. Whether something dissolves comes down to "like dissolves like".',
 'A solution is a solute dissolved in a solvent. Polar and ionic substances dissolve in polar solvents like water; nonpolar substances dissolve in nonpolar ones. Solubility depends on temperature, and on pressure for gases.',
 [['Like dissolves like', r'\text{polar or ionic in water; nonpolar in nonpolar}'], ['Saturation', r'\text{unsaturated, saturated, supersaturated}'], ['Gases', r'\text{more soluble cold and under pressure}']],
 ['Identify the solute (what is dissolved) and the solvent (what does the dissolving).', 'Compare polarity: like dissolves like.', 'Compare the amount dissolved with the solubility to judge saturation.', 'To find what crystallizes on cooling, subtract the two solubilities and scale for the mass of water.'],
 ['Stirring and crushing speed up dissolving but don\'t change how much can dissolve.', 'Gases are LESS soluble in warm water.', 'Solubility tables are per 100 g of water: scale for other amounts.'],
 'Think of the solvent as a gallery of fans. Polar water molecules love talking to charged or polar visitors (salt, sugar), so those mix right in. Oil is the quiet visitor who won\'t join the conversation, so it stays apart. Saturated means the gallery is full: every extra fan gets turned away.')
L['molarity'] = ('Molarity',
 'A sports-drink label tells you how much electrolyte is in each bottle. Molarity tells you how many moles are in each liter.',
 'Molarity (M) = moles of solute ÷ liters of solution.',
 [['Molarity', r'M=\f{\text{mol solute}}{\text{L solution}}'], ['Moles', r'n=M\times V'], ['Ion concentration', r'0.2\text{ M }\ce{CaCl2}\text{ gives }0.4\text{ M }\ce{Cl^-}']],
 ['Convert grams of solute to moles.', 'Convert mL to L (÷ 1000).', 'M = moles ÷ liters; rearrange to n = M × V or V = n ÷ M.', 'For one ion\'s concentration, multiply by how many of that ion are in each formula unit.'],
 ['The volume must be in liters.', 'Moles, not grams, go on top.', 'Count the ions per formula: 0.2 M CaCl₂ is 0.4 M in Cl⁻.'],
 'Molarity is like balls per bucket. A 2 M solution has 2 moles of solute in every liter, the way a large bucket always holds the same number of balls. Twice the volume holds twice the moles, but the concentration stays the same.')
L['dilute'] = ('Dilution',
 'Mixing sports-drink powder into more water spreads the same powder through a bigger bottle. Diluting never changes the moles of solute.',
 'Adding water doesn\'t change the moles of solute, so M₁V₁ = M₂V₂.',
 [['Dilution', r'M_1V_1=M_2V_2'], ['Water to add', r'V_2-V_1']],
 ['Label the concentrated solution 1 and the diluted solution 2.', 'Fill in the three known values.', 'Solve for the unknown, with both volumes in the same unit.', 'Water to add = final volume − starting volume.'],
 ['The concentrated stock needs a SMALLER volume than the final solution.', 'Diluting always lowers the molarity.', 'V₂ is the total final volume, not the water added.'],
 'Pour a bucket of 50 balls into a bigger bucket and you still have 50 balls (the same moles), just spread through more room (a lower concentration). M₁V₁ = M₂V₂ simply says "balls before = balls after".')
L['collig'] = ('Concentration units and colligative properties',
 'Ground crews salt icy paths because salt makes ice melt below 0 °C. Dissolved particles change freezing and boiling points.',
 'Colligative properties depend on the number of dissolved particles, not on what they are. ΔT = i·K·m, where m is molality and i is the number of particles per formula unit.',
 [['Percent by mass', r'\f{\text{mass solute}}{\text{mass solution}}\times100'], ['Molality', r'm=\f{\text{mol solute}}{\text{kg solvent}}'], ['Temperature change', r'\Delta T=i\,K\,m'], ['For water', r'K_f=1.86,\;K_b=0.512\text{ °C/m}']],
 ['Percent by mass: divide by the whole solution (solute + solvent).', 'Molality: moles of solute ÷ kilograms of solvent.', 'Find i: 1 for molecules like sugar, 2 for NaCl, 3 for CaCl₂.', 'ΔT = i K m. Subtract it from 0 °C for the freezing point; add it to 100 °C for the boiling point.'],
 ['Molality uses kilograms of SOLVENT, not liters of solution.', 'Ionic solutes split apart: don\'t forget i.', 'Solutes LOWER freezing points and RAISE boiling points.'],
 'Think of dissolved particles as people blocking the path. Water molecules trying to lock into ice keep bumping into them, so it has to get colder before ice can form. What matters is how many blockers there are, not who they are, and salt brings two per formula unit.')
L['acidbase'] = ('Acids, bases and conjugate pairs',
 'In an alternate-shot match, one player hits and hands off to the partner. In a Brønsted–Lowry reaction, an acid hands an H⁺ to a base.',
 'Arrhenius: acids make H⁺ in water, bases make OH⁻. Brønsted–Lowry: acids donate H⁺, bases accept it. Conjugate pairs differ by one H⁺. Strong acids and bases break apart completely in water.',
 [['Brønsted–Lowry acid', r'\text{donates H⁺}'], ['Brønsted–Lowry base', r'\text{accepts H⁺}'], ['Conjugate pairs', r'\ce{HCl}/\ce{Cl^-},\;\ce{NH4^+}/\ce{NH3}'], ['Strong acids', r'\text{HCl, HBr, HI, HNO₃, H₂SO₄, HClO₄}']],
 ['Find the H⁺ that moves: the giver is the acid; the receiver is the base.', 'Conjugate base = the acid minus one H⁺ (its charge drops by 1).', 'Conjugate acid = the base plus one H⁺ (its charge rises by 1).', 'Strong acids and bases ionize completely; all the others are weak.'],
 ['A conjugate pair differs by exactly ONE H⁺.', 'Water can act as an acid or as a base: it is amphoteric.', 'Weak doesn\'t mean dilute; it means only partly ionized.'],
 'Picture handing over the flag stick. The acid holds the H⁺ (the flag) and passes it to the base. After the hand-off, the base is holding it, so it has become the conjugate acid, and the old acid, now empty-handed, is the conjugate base, ready to take it back.')
L['ph'] = ('pH and pOH',
 'Golf scores and pH both run backward: a lower pH means MORE acidic, the way a lower score means better golf.',
 'pH = −log[H⁺], pOH = −log[OH⁻], and pH + pOH = 14 at 25 °C. Below 7 is acidic, 7 is neutral, above 7 is basic. Each pH unit is a tenfold change.',
 [['pH', r'\text{pH}=-\log[\text{H}^+]'], ['[H⁺] from pH', r'[\text{H}^+]=10^{-\text{pH}}'], ['pH and pOH', r'\text{pH}+\text{pOH}=14'], ['Strong bases', r'[\text{OH}^-]=M\times(\text{OH}^-\text{ per formula})']],
 ['From [H⁺]: pH = −log[H⁺].', 'From pH: [H⁺] = 10 to the power −pH.', 'From [OH⁻]: find pOH = −log[OH⁻], then pH = 14 − pOH.', 'Strong acids give [H⁺] equal to their concentration; strong bases give [OH⁻] (twice it for Ca(OH)₂).'],
 ['Use log base 10, not ln.', 'For a base, find pOH first, then subtract from 14.', 'pH 3 is ten times more acidic than pH 4.'],
 'The pH scale is like a handicap for acidity: lower means stronger. Each step down by one is ten times more acidic, the way a few strokes of handicap mark a very different player. Pure water sits at 7: neutral, like scratch.')
L['titrate'] = ('Neutralization and titration',
 'A match is all square when both sides have won the same number of holes. A titration is complete when the moles of H⁺ equal the moles of OH⁻.',
 'Acid + base → salt + water. At the equivalence point, moles of H⁺ from the acid equal moles of OH⁻ from the base: (H⁺ per acid) MₐVₐ = (OH⁻ per base) M_bV_b.',
 [['Neutralization', r'\ce{HCl + NaOH -> NaCl + H2O}'], ['1 : 1 acids and bases', r'M_aV_a=M_bV_b'], ['H₂SO₄ with NaOH', r'2M_aV_a=M_bV_b']],
 ['Write the neutralization: H⁺ + OH⁻ → H₂O, and the leftover ions make the salt.', 'Count the H⁺ per acid formula and the OH⁻ per base formula.', 'Set moles of H⁺ equal to moles of OH⁻.', 'Solve for the unknown concentration or volume.'],
 ['H₂SO₄ gives 2 H⁺ and Ca(OH)₂ gives 2 OH⁻: include them.', 'The products are a salt and WATER, not hydrogen gas.', 'Volumes can stay in mL as long as both sides use mL.'],
 'Think of the titration as squaring a match: every H⁺ the acid puts up must be matched by an OH⁻ from the base. When the match is all square (equal moles), the indicator changes color. A diprotic acid like H₂SO₄ wins two holes per shot, so it needs twice the base.')
L['heat'] = ('Specific heat and calorimetry',
 'A metal club head gets hot fast in the sun, but a water bottle stays cool much longer. Water has a huge specific heat.',
 'q = mcΔT: heat equals mass × specific heat × temperature change. In a calorimeter, the heat lost by the hot object equals the heat gained by the cold one.',
 [['Heat', r'q=mc\Delta T'], ['Water', r'c=4.18\text{ J/(g·°C)}'], ['Calorimetry', r'q_{\text{lost}}=q_{\text{gained}}']],
 ['Find ΔT = final − initial.', 'Multiply m × c × ΔT to get joules (÷ 1000 for kJ).', 'Rearrange to find c, m or ΔT; final temperature = start + ΔT.', 'Calorimetry: m_m c_m (T_m − T_f) = m_w c_w (T_f − T_w).'],
 ['Use the temperature CHANGE, not the final temperature.', 'Endothermic processes absorb heat (the surroundings feel cold); exothermic ones release it.', '1 kJ = 1000 J.'],
 'Specific heat is like a player\'s temperament. Water is the calm veteran: pour in a lot of energy and its temperature barely moves. Metals are hotheads: a little energy and their temperature jumps. In a calorimeter, the hot metal loses exactly as much energy as the water gains.')
L['enthalpy'] = ('Enthalpy and phase changes',
 'On a heating curve, the flat stretches are like waiting on the tee: energy keeps going in, but the temperature doesn\'t move.',
 'ΔH is the heat of a reaction as written; a negative ΔH means heat is released. During a phase change the temperature stays constant: q = mΔH_fus for melting or mΔH_vap for boiling.',
 [['Thermochemistry', r'\Delta H\text{ goes with the coefficients as written}'], ['Phase change', r'q=m\Delta H'], ['Water', r'\Delta H_{\text{fus}}=334\text{ J/g},\;\Delta H_{\text{vap}}=2260\text{ J/g}']],
 ['Reaction heat: grams → moles, then × ΔH ÷ that substance\'s coefficient.', 'Melting or freezing: q = m × ΔH_fus. Boiling or condensing: q = m × ΔH_vap.', 'Sloped parts of a heating curve use q = mcΔT; flat parts use q = mΔH.', 'For a multi-step change, add the heat for each segment.'],
 ['ΔH matches the coefficients: −572 kJ is for 2 mol of H₂.', 'Melting uses ΔH_fus; boiling uses ΔH_vap.', 'Don\'t skip the melting step when warming ice into water.'],
 'Picture a heating curve as a round with waits. On the sloped holes, the temperature climbs as you add heat. On the flat stretches you are waiting on the tee: energy still goes in, but it breaks the attractions between particles (melting or boiling) instead of raising the temperature.')
L['nuclear'] = ('Nuclear chemistry',
 'If half your range balls vanished into the trees every week, after four weeks you would have one-sixteenth left. That is half-life.',
 'Unstable nuclei decay by giving off alpha particles (⁴₂He), beta particles (⁰₋₁e), positrons (⁰₊₁e) or gamma rays. Mass numbers and atomic numbers balance in nuclear equations. A half-life is the time for half a sample to decay.',
 [['Alpha decay', r'\text{A − 4, Z − 2}'], ['Beta decay', r'\text{A same, Z + 1}'], ['Half-life', r'\text{left}=\text{start}\times\left(\f{1}{2}\right)^{n}'], ['Penetration', r'\text{alpha < beta < gamma}']],
 ['Balance mass numbers (top) and atomic numbers (bottom) on both sides.', 'Use the new atomic number to name the new element.', 'Half-life: n = time ÷ half-life, and remaining = start ÷ 2ⁿ.', 'Fission splits heavy nuclei (nuclear power plants); fusion joins light nuclei (the Sun).'],
 ['Half-lives halve repeatedly: after 3 half-lives, 1/8 is left, not 1/6.', 'Beta decay RAISES the atomic number by 1.', 'Gamma rays don\'t change the mass number or atomic number.'],
 'Imagine a bucket of 80 balls where half go missing every week. After one week, 40; two weeks, 20; three, 10. You never reach zero in a straight line; you keep halving. Each nucleus decays at random, like any one ball, but the whole bucket follows the halving pattern.')

# Concept bank: (skill, difficulty, question, [right, wrong1, wrong2, wrong3], [why1, why2, why3], hint, explanation)
B = [
 ('matter', 1, 'Which can be separated by physical means, such as filtering or evaporating?', ['A mixture', 'An element', 'A compound', 'Any pure substance'], ['An element is one kind of atom; it can\'t be broken down at all.', 'A compound can only be broken apart by a chemical change.', 'Pure substances can\'t be separated physically.'], 'Mixtures keep their parts\' identities.', 'The parts of a mixture keep their own properties, so physical methods can separate them.'),
 ('matter', 2, 'A student heats a white powder. It turns black and gives off a gas. What does this show?', ['A chemical change: new substances formed', 'A physical change: the powder melted', 'A physical change: the powder dissolved', 'No change: the powder only got hot'], ['Melting doesn\'t change color to black or release a new gas.', 'Nothing was dissolved.', 'A color change and a new gas are evidence of new substances.'], 'Look for evidence of new substances.', 'A color change plus a gas being produced are signs of a chemical change.'),
 ('sigfig', 1, 'Three measurements of a 5.00 g mass read 4.12 g, 4.11 g and 4.13 g. They are…', ['Precise but not accurate', 'Accurate but not precise', 'Both accurate and precise', 'Neither accurate nor precise'], ['They are far from the true 5.00 g, so they aren\'t accurate.', 'They are close to the true value only if they average near 5.00 g; they don\'t.', 'They agree with each other but miss the true value.'], 'Precision: close to each other. Accuracy: close to the true value.', 'The readings agree closely (precise) but are all about 0.9 g off (not accurate).'),
 ('sigfig', 2, 'Why does 0.0050 have two significant figures?', ['The leading zeros only place the decimal point; the trailing zero after the decimal is measured', 'All zeros are significant', 'Only nonzero digits are significant', 'Zeros after a decimal point never count'], ['Leading zeros never count.', 'The trailing zero after the decimal point does count.', 'Trailing zeros after a decimal point do count: they show precision.'], 'Separate the leading zeros from the trailing zero.', 'The "5" and the final "0" are significant; the zeros in front are placeholders.'),
 ('convert', 1, 'Which is the largest length?', ['1 km', '1000 cm', '1000 mm', '100 m'], ['1000 cm is 10 m.', '1000 mm is 1 m.', '100 m is a tenth of a kilometer.'], 'Convert each to meters.', '1 km = 1000 m, larger than 100 m, 10 m or 1 m.'),
 ('convert', 2, 'Why can a conversion factor such as 1000 m / 1 km be multiplied without changing the length?', ['It equals 1, because the top and bottom are the same length', 'It makes the number bigger', 'It only changes the significant figures', 'It changes kilometers into a different quantity'], ['It makes the NUMBER bigger, but the length is unchanged.', 'Exact conversion factors don\'t affect significant figures.', 'It changes only the unit, not the quantity being measured.'], 'What is 1000 m divided by 1 km?', '1000 m and 1 km are the same distance, so their ratio is exactly 1.'),
 ('density', 1, 'An object floats in water (density 1.00 g/mL). What do you know about its density?', ['It is less than 1.00 g/mL', 'It is more than 1.00 g/mL', 'It is exactly 1.00 g/mL', 'Floating says nothing about density'], ['A denser object would sink.', 'Equal density would make it hover, neither rising nor sinking.', 'Floating or sinking is decided by density.'], 'Compare the object with the liquid.', 'Less dense objects float on denser liquids.'),
 ('density', 2, 'A block of wood is cut in half. What happens to the density of each half?', ['It stays the same', 'It is halved', 'It doubles', 'It depends on which way you cut it'], ['Both mass and volume are halved, so their ratio is unchanged.', 'Density doesn\'t depend on amount.', 'Density is an intensive property.'], 'What happens to mass and volume?', 'Mass and volume both halve, so D = m/V is unchanged: density is intensive.'),
 ('models', 2, 'Why did Rutherford conclude that the nucleus is positive?', ['Positive alpha particles were strongly repelled and bounced back', 'Electrons were attracted to the foil', 'Most alpha particles went straight through', 'The foil glowed when it was hit'], ['The experiment tracked alpha particles, not electrons.', 'That showed the atom is mostly empty space, not that the nucleus is positive.', 'The glow on the detector screen just recorded where the particles landed.'], 'Like charges repel.', 'Only a concentrated positive charge could repel positive alpha particles so strongly.'),
 ('models', 1, 'What did J.J. Thomson\'s cathode-ray experiments show?', ['Atoms contain small negative particles (electrons)', 'Atoms have a dense nucleus', 'Electrons orbit in fixed energy levels', 'Neutrons exist in the nucleus'], ['The nucleus came from Rutherford\'s gold-foil experiment.', 'Energy levels were Bohr\'s idea.', 'Neutrons were found by Chadwick in 1932.'], 'Cathode rays bent toward a positive plate.', 'Cathode rays were deflected by charged plates, showing negative particles inside atoms.'),
 ('isotope', 1, 'Carbon-12 and carbon-14 have different numbers of…', ['Neutrons', 'Protons', 'Electrons in the neutral atom', 'Valence electrons'], ['Same element, same protons (6).', 'Neutral isotopes have the same electrons.', 'Valence electrons are the same for isotopes of an element.'], 'The number is the mass number.', 'Both have 6 protons; carbon-14 has 8 neutrons and carbon-12 has 6.'),
 ('isotope', 2, 'Which change turns a neutral atom into a different element?', ['Adding a proton', 'Adding a neutron', 'Removing an electron', 'Adding an electron'], ['That makes an isotope of the same element.', 'That makes a positive ion of the same element.', 'That makes a negative ion of the same element.'], 'What sets an element\'s identity?', 'The number of protons (the atomic number) identifies the element.'),
 ('avgmass', 1, 'Copper is 69% copper-63 and 31% copper-65. Its average atomic mass is closest to…', ['63.5', '64.0', '65.0', '63.0'], ['64.0 would mean equal amounts of each isotope.', 'That is the less common isotope.', 'Some copper-65 pulls the average above 63.'], 'The average is pulled toward the more common isotope.', '0.69(63) + 0.31(65) ≈ 63.6, close to 63.5.'),
 ('avgmass', 2, 'Why aren\'t the atomic masses on the periodic table whole numbers?', ['They are weighted averages of the masses of an element\'s isotopes', 'Scientists measure them inaccurately', 'Electrons add fractional amounts of mass', 'Protons and neutrons have different charges'], ['They are measured very precisely.', 'Electrons barely affect the mass.', 'Charge doesn\'t set mass.'], 'Think about isotopes.', 'Each element is a mix of isotopes, so its average mass falls between whole numbers.'),
 ('light', 1, 'Why does each element give a unique line spectrum?', ['Its electrons can only jump between its own set of energy levels', 'Each element has a different color of nucleus', 'Its atoms vibrate at different speeds', 'Each element absorbs all colors equally'], ['A nucleus doesn\'t have a color.', 'Line spectra come from electron transitions, not vibration.', 'Then there would be no distinct lines.'], 'Think about electrons falling between levels.', 'Each element\'s energy levels are different, so the photons emitted (the lines) are different.'),
 ('light', 2, 'When an electron falls from a higher to a lower energy level, the atom…', ['Emits a photon of light', 'Absorbs a photon of light', 'Loses a proton', 'Becomes an ion'], ['Absorbing light moves an electron UP.', 'Electron transitions don\'t change the nucleus.', 'The electron stays in the atom.'], 'Energy has to go somewhere.', 'The energy difference is released as a photon.'),
 ('config', 1, "Hund's rule says that electrons in the same sublevel…", ['Fill each orbital singly before pairing up', 'Always pair up first', 'Must have the same spin in one orbital', 'Fill the 4s sublevel last'], ['They spread out before pairing.', 'Two electrons in one orbital have opposite spins (Pauli).', '4s fills before 3d.'], 'Think of seats on a bus.', 'Like passengers taking empty seats first, electrons occupy empty orbitals before pairing.'),
 ('config', 2, 'How many electrons can one orbital hold?', ['2, with opposite spins', '1', '6', '8'], ['Each orbital holds a pair.', 'That is a whole p sublevel (3 orbitals).', 'That is an octet, many orbitals.'], 'Pauli exclusion principle.', 'An orbital holds at most two electrons, and they must have opposite spins.'),
 ('table', 1, 'Elements in the same group have similar properties because they have the same…', ['Number of valence electrons', 'Atomic mass', 'Number of neutrons', 'Number of energy levels'], ['Atomic mass increases down a group.', 'Neutrons vary widely.', 'Energy levels increase down a group.'], 'What does chemistry depend on?', 'Valence electrons decide how atoms react, and a group shares the same number.'),
 ('table', 2, 'Moseley rearranged the periodic table by…', ['Atomic number', 'Atomic mass', 'Date of discovery', 'Number of neutrons'], ['Mendeleev used atomic mass; Moseley fixed it with atomic number.', 'Discovery date has nothing to do with properties.', 'Neutron counts aren\'t a property of the element itself.'], 'Moseley measured X-rays.', 'Ordering by atomic number put every element in a group with similar properties.'),
 ('trends', 2, 'Why does atomic radius decrease across a period?', ['More protons pull the same energy level closer', 'Electrons are removed across the period', 'Neutrons push electrons inward', 'New energy levels are added'], ['Atoms gain electrons across a period.', 'Neutrons have no charge.', 'The energy level stays the same across a period.'], 'What increases across a period?', 'The nuclear charge grows while the shielding stays about the same, so electrons are pulled in.'),
 ('trends', 1, 'Which group has the highest ionization energies in each period?', ['The noble gases (group 18)', 'The alkali metals (group 1)', 'The transition metals', 'The halogens (group 17)'], ['Alkali metals have the LOWEST.', 'Transition metals are in the middle.', 'Halogens are high but the noble gases are higher.'], 'Which atoms hold their electrons most tightly?', 'A full outer level makes noble gases very hard to ionize.'),
 ('ionic', 1, 'Which property is typical of ionic compounds?', ['High melting points and conducting electricity when dissolved', 'Low melting points', 'Conducting electricity as solids', 'Being soft and flexible'], ['Strong attractions between ions mean high melting points.', 'Ions are locked in place in a solid.', 'Ionic crystals are hard and brittle.'], 'Think about a crystal of ions.', 'Strong ionic attractions give high melting points; dissolved or molten ions can move and carry charge.'),
 ('ionic', 2, 'Metals conduct electricity because…', ['Their valence electrons are free to move through the metal', 'They contain ions that move as a liquid', 'Their protons move', 'They have covalent bonds'], ['A solid metal doesn\'t flow.', 'Protons stay in the nuclei.', 'Metallic bonding is a "sea" of shared electrons.'], 'Sea of electrons.', 'In metallic bonding, valence electrons move freely, carrying charge.'),
 ('lewis', 2, 'Which atom is often an exception to the octet rule with only six electrons?', ['Boron in BF₃', 'Carbon in CH₄', 'Nitrogen in NH₃', 'Oxygen in H₂O'], ['Carbon has a full octet in CH₄.', 'Nitrogen has a full octet in NH₃.', 'Oxygen has a full octet in H₂O.'], 'Group 13.', 'Boron has only three valence electrons and forms three bonds, ending with six.'),
 ('lewis', 1, 'A double bond is…', ['Two shared pairs of electrons', 'Two lone pairs', 'One shared pair', 'Two electrons transferred'], ['Lone pairs aren\'t shared.', 'That is a single bond.', 'Transferring electrons makes ions.'], 'Count shared pairs.', 'Each bond is a shared pair, so a double bond shares two pairs (four electrons).'),
 ('shape', 2, 'CO₂ has two polar C=O bonds. Why is the molecule nonpolar?', ['It is linear, so the bond dipoles cancel', 'Carbon and oxygen have equal electronegativity', 'It has lone pairs on carbon', 'Double bonds are never polar'], ['Oxygen is more electronegative than carbon.', 'Carbon has no lone pairs in CO₂.', 'The bonds are polar; the shape cancels them.'], 'Look at the shape.', 'Equal pulls in opposite directions cancel out.'),
 ('shape', 1, 'What does VSEPR theory say electron groups do?', ['Repel each other and spread as far apart as possible', 'Attract each other into pairs', 'Line up in a straight row', 'Stay on one side of the atom'], ['Electron groups are all negative, so they repel.', 'They spread in three dimensions.', 'They spread out evenly.'], 'Like charges repel.', 'Bonds and lone pairs push apart, which sets the molecule\'s shape.'),
 ('imf', 1, 'Which describes forces BETWEEN molecules?', ['Intermolecular forces', 'Covalent bonds', 'Ionic bonds', 'Nuclear forces'], ['Covalent bonds hold atoms together inside a molecule.', 'Ionic bonds hold ions in a crystal.', 'Nuclear forces hold the nucleus together.'], '"Inter" means between.', 'Intermolecular forces act between separate molecules.'),
 ('imf', 2, 'Water has a high surface tension because…', ['Hydrogen bonds pull surface molecules inward', 'Water molecules are nonpolar', 'Water has very weak attractions', 'Water molecules are very large'], ['Water is polar.', 'Its hydrogen bonds are strong for its size.', 'Water molecules are small.'], 'Think about hydrogen bonding.', 'Strong hydrogen bonds hold the surface molecules together like a stretched skin.'),
 ('nameion', 1, 'Why does CuCl₂ need a Roman numeral in its name, copper(II) chloride?', ['Copper can form more than one ion (Cu⁺ and Cu²⁺)', 'There are two chlorine atoms', 'Copper is a nonmetal', 'All compounds need Roman numerals'], ['The numeral shows the metal\'s charge, not the number of chlorines.', 'Copper is a metal.', 'Metals with one possible charge (Na, Ca) don\'t get one.'], 'What is the numeral for?', 'The Roman numeral tells which copper ion is present: here Cu²⁺.'),
 ('nameion', 2, 'What is the name of NH₄NO₃?', ['Ammonium nitrate', 'Nitrogen hydrogen nitrate', 'Ammonium nitride', 'Ammonia nitrate'], ['NH₄⁺ is the ammonium ion.', 'NO₃⁻ is nitrate; nitride is N³⁻.', 'Ammonia is NH₃; the ion is ammonium.'], 'Both parts are polyatomic ions.', 'NH₄⁺ is ammonium and NO₃⁻ is nitrate.'),
 ('namecov', 1, 'Why do molecular compounds use prefixes like di- and tri-?', ['Nonmetals can combine in several ratios, so the name must state the numbers', 'Molecular compounds contain ions', 'Prefixes show the charges', 'Prefixes show the state of matter'], ['Molecular compounds don\'t contain ions.', 'Molecules have no ion charges to show.', 'States are shown with (s), (l), (g), (aq).'], 'Think of CO and CO₂.', 'CO and CO₂ are different compounds, so the name must say how many oxygens.'),
 ('namecov', 2, 'Which is the correct name for H₂SO₄(aq)?', ['Sulfuric acid', 'Hydrosulfuric acid', 'Sulfurous acid', 'Hydrogen sulfide'], ['Hydro- is only for acids without oxygen.', 'Sulfurous acid is H₂SO₃ (from sulfite).', 'Hydrogen sulfide is H₂S.'], 'Sulfate becomes…?', 'The anion is sulfate (-ate), so the acid is sulfuric (-ic).'),
 ('balance', 1, 'Why must chemical equations be balanced?', ['Atoms are neither created nor destroyed in a reaction', 'Reactants and products must have the same number of molecules', 'Coefficients must all be equal', 'The masses of individual substances stay the same'], ['The number of molecules can change.', 'Coefficients are often different.', 'Individual substances change; only the total mass is conserved.'], 'Law of conservation of mass.', 'Every atom present at the start must still be there at the end.'),
 ('balance', 2, 'A student balances H₂ + O₂ → H₂O by changing it to H₂ + O₂ → H₂O₂. What went wrong?', ['They changed a subscript, which made a different substance', 'They used too many coefficients', 'Nothing: it is balanced', 'They should have changed O₂ to O'], ['They used no coefficients at all.', 'H₂O₂ is hydrogen peroxide, not water.', 'Oxygen gas is O₂; changing it changes the substance.'], 'What do subscripts mean?', 'Only coefficients may change; the correct balance is 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O.'),
 ('rxtype', 1, 'Which is evidence of a chemical reaction?', ['Bubbles of gas form when two liquids mix', 'Ice melts on the counter', 'Sugar dissolves in water', 'A glass breaks'], ['Melting is a physical change.', 'Dissolving is a physical change.', 'Breaking is a physical change.'], 'Look for a new substance.', 'Unexpected gas formation is a sign that a new substance formed.'),
 ('rxtype', 2, 'Will Cu react with ZnCl₂ solution? Zinc is more active than copper.', ['No, copper can\'t replace the more active zinc', 'Yes, copper replaces zinc', 'Yes, they combine into one compound', 'Yes, but only if heated'], ['Only a more active metal can replace another.', 'Single replacement doesn\'t combine everything.', 'Heating doesn\'t change the activity order.'], 'Activity series.', 'A metal replaces only metals below it on the activity series; copper is below zinc.'),
 ('molar', 1, 'Which contains more atoms: 1 mol of helium or 1 mol of neon?', ['They contain the same number', 'Helium', 'Neon', 'It depends on the temperature'], ['One mole is always 6.02 × 10²³ particles.', 'One mole is always the same count.', 'The count doesn\'t depend on conditions.'], 'A mole is a count.', 'One mole of anything is 6.02 × 10²³ particles.'),
 ('molar', 2, 'Which has the greater mass: 1 mol of CO₂ or 1 mol of H₂O?', ['1 mol of CO₂ (44 g)', '1 mol of H₂O (18 g)', 'They have the same mass', 'It can\'t be determined'], ['Water\'s molar mass is smaller.', 'Same count, different molar masses.', 'Their molar masses are known.'], 'Compare molar masses.', 'Same number of molecules, but each CO₂ is heavier.'),
 ('pcomp', 1, 'Two samples of pure water, 10 g and 100 g, are analyzed. Their percent oxygen is…', ['The same, 88.8%', 'Ten times bigger for the 100 g sample', 'Ten times smaller for the 100 g sample', 'Different, depending on where the water came from'], ['Percent composition doesn\'t depend on the amount.', 'Percent composition doesn\'t depend on the amount.', 'A pure compound has a fixed composition.'], 'Law of definite proportions.', 'A compound always has the same percent composition by mass.'),
 ('empir', 1, 'Which pair has the same empirical formula?', ['C₂H₂ and C₆H₆', 'CO and CO₂', 'H₂O and H₂O₂', 'CH₄ and C₂H₆'], ['CO is 1:1 and CO₂ is 1:2.', 'H₂O is 2:1 and H₂O₂ is 1:1.', 'CH₄ is 1:4 and C₂H₆ is 1:3.'], 'Reduce each formula.', 'Both reduce to CH.'),
 ('stoich', 1, 'In 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O, how many molecules of O₂ react with 10 molecules of H₂?', ['5', '10', '20', '2'], ['The ratio is 2 H₂ : 1 O₂.', 'That would need twice the H₂.', 'Use the ratio of coefficients, not the coefficient.'], 'Use the coefficients.', '2 H₂ for every 1 O₂, so 10 H₂ need 5 O₂.'),
 ('stoich', 2, 'Total mass before a reaction in a sealed container is 50.0 g. After the reaction it is…', ['50.0 g', 'More than 50.0 g, if a gas forms', 'Less than 50.0 g, if a gas forms', 'It depends on the mole ratio'], ['Mass is conserved in a sealed container.', 'The gas is still inside the sealed container.', 'Mole ratios don\'t change total mass.'], 'Conservation of mass.', 'In a closed system, mass is conserved.'),
 ('limit', 1, 'A sandwich needs 2 slices of bread and 1 slice of cheese. You have 10 slices of bread and 7 slices of cheese. What limits the sandwiches?', ['The bread (5 sandwiches)', 'The cheese (7 sandwiches)', 'Neither, you can make 17', 'Both run out together'], ['7 cheese could make 7, but the bread runs out at 5.', 'Each sandwich uses both.', '10 bread makes 5; 7 cheese makes 7.'], 'How many sandwiches could each make?', 'The bread runs out after 5 sandwiches, so it is limiting.'),
 ('pressure', 2, 'A sealed bottle of air is warmed. Why does its pressure rise?', ['The particles move faster and hit the walls harder and more often', 'The particles get bigger', 'More particles are made', 'The particles attract each other more'], ['Particle size doesn\'t change.', 'No particles are added.', 'Attractions don\'t increase pressure.'], 'Temperature is average kinetic energy.', 'Faster particles collide more often and with more force.'),
 ('gaslaw', 2, 'A balloon is taken from a warm house out into freezing air. It shrinks because…', ['Lower temperature means lower volume at the same pressure', 'Gas escapes through the balloon', 'The air outside has higher pressure', 'Cold air makes the gas heavier'], ['The amount of gas is the same.', 'Air pressure is about the same indoors and out.', 'Mass doesn\'t change with temperature.'], "Charles's law.", 'Volume is proportional to kelvin temperature.'),
 ('ideal', 1, 'Under which conditions does a real gas behave most like an ideal gas?', ['High temperature and low pressure', 'Low temperature and high pressure', 'Low temperature and low pressure', 'High pressure only'], ['Then attractions and particle size matter more.', 'Low temperature makes attractions matter.', 'High pressure crowds the particles.'], 'When are attractions least important?', 'Fast, far-apart particles hardly feel each other, matching the ideal-gas assumptions.'),
 ('dalton', 1, 'Air is about 78% nitrogen. At a total pressure of 1.00 atm, the partial pressure of nitrogen is about…', ['0.78 atm', '1.00 atm', '0.22 atm', '78 atm'], ['That is the total pressure.', 'That is everything except nitrogen.', 'Use the fraction 0.78, not 78.'], 'Partial pressure = fraction × total.', '0.78 × 1.00 atm = 0.78 atm.'),
 ('solub', 2, 'Which solution conducts electricity best?', ['Salt (NaCl) dissolved in water', 'Sugar dissolved in water', 'Pure water', 'Ethanol dissolved in water'], ['Sugar doesn\'t form ions.', 'Pure water has very few ions.', 'Ethanol doesn\'t form ions.'], 'Which solute makes ions?', 'NaCl dissociates into Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions that carry current: it is an electrolyte.'),
 ('molarity', 1, 'What does "2.0 M NaCl" mean?', ['2.0 mol of NaCl in every liter of solution', '2.0 g of NaCl in every liter', '2.0 mol of NaCl in every liter of water added', '2.0 mol of NaCl in every kilogram of water'], ['M counts moles, not grams.', 'It is per liter of SOLUTION, not of water.', 'That is molality.'], 'Molarity = mol per L of solution.', 'Molarity is moles of solute per liter of solution.'),
 ('dilute', 1, 'You dilute 100 mL of a solution to 200 mL. The concentration…', ['Halves', 'Doubles', 'Stays the same', 'Drops to a quarter'], ['Adding water spreads the solute out.', 'The moles stay the same in a bigger volume.', 'Doubling the volume halves the concentration.'], 'M₁V₁ = M₂V₂.', 'Same moles in twice the volume: half the concentration.'),
 ('collig', 2, 'Why is CaCl₂ better than NaCl for melting ice on sidewalks?', ['Each CaCl₂ gives 3 ions instead of 2, lowering the freezing point more', 'CaCl₂ is a stronger acid', 'CaCl₂ reacts with water to make heat only', 'NaCl doesn\'t dissolve in water'], ['Neither is an acid.', 'Its main effect is colligative.', 'NaCl dissolves well.'], 'Count the particles.', 'More dissolved particles mean a bigger freezing-point drop.'),
 ('acidbase', 1, 'In NH₃ + H₂O → NH₄⁺ + OH⁻, water acts as…', ['An acid: it donates H⁺', 'A base: it accepts H⁺', 'Neither', 'A salt'], ['Water gives its H⁺ to ammonia here.', 'Water takes part in the H⁺ transfer.', 'No salt forms.'], 'Follow the H⁺.', 'Water donates H⁺ to NH₃, so it acts as a Brønsted–Lowry acid.'),
 ('ph', 1, 'A solution with pH 3 compared with a solution with pH 5 has…', ['100 times more H⁺', '2 times more H⁺', '100 times less H⁺', 'The same H⁺'], ['Each pH unit is a factor of 10.', 'A lower pH has MORE H⁺.', 'Different pH means different [H⁺].'], 'Each pH unit is ×10.', 'Two pH units means 10 × 10 = 100 times more H⁺.'),
 ('ph', 2, 'Adding a base to an acidic solution makes the pH…', ['Rise toward 7', 'Fall', 'Stay the same', 'Become negative'], ['A base removes H⁺, so the pH goes up.', 'The base neutralizes H⁺.', 'Adding base can\'t make pH negative.'], 'Bases neutralize H⁺.', 'Removing H⁺ raises the pH.'),
 ('titrate', 1, 'What does an indicator\'s color change mark in a titration?', ['The endpoint, where the acid and base are neutralized', 'The start of the reaction', 'The point where the acid is strongest', 'When the solution boils'], ['The color changes at the end.', 'It marks neutralization, not strength.', 'Titrations aren\'t heated to boiling.'], 'When do you stop adding base?', 'The indicator changes color when the moles of acid and base match.'),
 ('heat', 1, 'Which absorbs the most heat for each 1 °C rise, per gram?', ['Water (c = 4.18 J/(g·°C))', 'Aluminum (c = 0.897)', 'Iron (c = 0.449)', 'Copper (c = 0.385)'], ['Aluminum\'s specific heat is lower.', 'Iron\'s is lower.', 'Copper\'s is the lowest here.'], 'Compare specific heats.', 'The higher the specific heat, the more heat per gram per degree.'),
 ('heat', 2, 'A reaction in a beaker makes the beaker feel cold. The reaction is…', ['Endothermic: it absorbs heat from the surroundings', 'Exothermic: it releases heat', 'Neither: no energy changes', 'Exothermic, because cold means energy left'], ['An exothermic reaction would warm the beaker.', 'A temperature change means energy moved.', 'Heat flowed INTO the reaction from your hand and the beaker.'], 'Which way does heat flow?', 'The reaction pulled heat from the surroundings, so they cooled.'),
 ('enthalpy', 1, 'A reaction has ΔH = −250 kJ. This means…', ['It releases 250 kJ of heat (exothermic)', 'It absorbs 250 kJ of heat', 'It needs 250 kJ to start', 'Its products have more energy'], ['A negative ΔH means heat is released.', 'That is activation energy, not ΔH.', 'The products have LESS energy.'], 'What does the minus sign mean?', 'Negative ΔH: heat leaves the system.'),
 ('nuclear', 2, 'Which change happens in beta decay?', ['A neutron turns into a proton and an electron is emitted', 'A proton turns into a neutron', 'A helium nucleus is emitted', 'Only energy is emitted'], ['That happens in positron emission.', 'That is alpha decay.', 'That is gamma emission.'], 'Beta particles are electrons.', 'The atomic number rises by 1 while the mass number stays the same.'),
]

